  "info": {
    "indicator_code": "30771724",
    "describe": "The core Personal Consumption Expenditures (PCE) price index is an inflation gauge compiled by the Bureau of Economic Analysis of the US Department of Commerce; it removes the volatility of food and energy prices to give a more stable picture of the underlying inflation trend. The data are seasonally adjusted, removing the disturbance of seasonal factors such as holidays and weather and making data across periods more comparable. The year-on-year measure compares the current month's index with the same period a year earlier, reflecting the annual change in prices. Core PCE is the inflation reference the Federal Reserve relies on most when setting monetary policy, and its long-term target of 2% directly affects the Fed's decisions on rate hikes or cuts. The trajectory of this data has a notable influence on US Treasury yields, the dollar exchange rate, and the equity market, making it an important basis for the market in judging the Federal Reserve's policy path.",
